</tbody>
</table></div>
<div class="p text-right font-secondary"><a href="table/tbl0005/" class="usa-link" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Open in a new tab</a></div></section><p id="p0125">The encapsulated array was attached to a Sono403 Multi-Purpose Phantom for imaging, with the focal depth set at 60 mm. Notably, no ultrasonic coupling agent or manual compression was applied during the procedure. <a href="#fig0035" class="usa-link">Fig. 7</a>(a) illustrates the ultrasound Point-Spread Functions (PSFs) obtained from wire targets at various depths. The corresponding lateral and axial resolutions are plotted in <a href="#fig0035" class="usa-link">Fig. 7</a>(b); it is observed that the lateral resolution first decreases and then increases, with the minimum value occurring at the 60 mm focal point, while the axial resolution remains essentially constant.</p>
<figure class="fig xbox font-sm" id="fig0035"><h4 class="obj_head">Fig. 7.</h4>
<p class="img-box line-height-none margin-x-neg-2 tablet:margin-x-0 text-center"><img class="graphic" src="https://cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/blobs/c1a5/13594705/8aab4eefaee1/gr7.webp" loading="lazy" id="lk0035" height="1564" width="1600" alt="Fig. 7"></p>
<div class="p text-right font-secondary"><a href="figure/fig0035/" class="usa-link" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Open in a new tab</a></div>
<figcaption><p>Imaging performance of the CCUA-Patch on the phantom. (a) Image of the L1 array on the top surface of the phantom with self-adhesive hydrogel. (b) Imaging resolution of a representative array at different depths. (c) Reconstructed image from L1 and L2 arrays and (d) image from H1 array on the tissue-mimicking phantom with hydrogel. (e) and (f) The automatic annotation results of the bladder contour and dimensions.</p></figcaption></figure><p id="p0130">Subsequently, imaging was performed on an ultrasound tissue-mimicking phantom (KS215BP, Institute of Acoustics, Chinese Academy of Sciences, China), again without the use of couplant or manual pressure. Following feature-based image stitching, the cross-sectional images are shown in <a href="#fig0035" class="usa-link">Fig. 7</a>(c) and (d), while <a href="#fig0035" class="usa-link">Fig. 7</a>(e) and (f) present the results of edge extraction and the three-diameter calculation. The measured diameters were 54.57 mm, 81.18 mm, and 54.76 mm, respectively, resulting in a calculated bladder volume of 126.14 mL. Compared to the phantom’s calibrated ovoid target volume of 137 mL, the absolute error was 10.86 mL, with a relative error of approximately 7.93%. For comparison, the three-diameter measurements obtained using a commercial handheld convex probe were 54.41 mm, 54.59 mm, and 81.09 mm, respectively, corresponding to a volume of 125.25 mL. The volume discrepancy between the CCUA-Patch and the commercial probe was only 0.89 mL.</p></section><section id="sec0050"><h3 class="pmc_sec_title">3.3. In vivo bladder volume monitoring</h3>
<p id="p0135">To evaluate the reliability of the CCUA-Patch in clinical applications, a volunteer study was conducted focusing on bladder volume monitoring under different filling states. Ten healthy volunteers (6 males and 4 females) aged 23–26, with a BMI ranging from 18.67 to 23.74 kg/m<sup>2</sup>, were recruited. The experiment was divided into two groups: Case I targeted the distended bladder, involving 3 males and 2 females who provided bladder images while experiencing a strong urge to urinate; Case II targeted the small bladder, involving the remaining 3 males and 2 females, with bladder images captured approximately 10 min post-urination when no urge was felt even under light pressure. After image processing and bladder contour identification, the spatial measurement accuracy of the system was validated by comparing the length, height, width, and estimated volume results of the CCUA-Patch against those obtained from a commercial Stork handheld ultrasound probe.</p>
<p id="p0140"><a href="#fig0040" class="usa-link">Fig. 8</a> and <a href="#fig0045" class="usa-link">Fig. 9</a> display the bladder images in these two states. For the distended bladders in Case I, two longitudinal images were stitched and annotated with bladder diameters, and the bladder volumes were calculated using the formula (1). Results showed the male bladder volumes of 410.20 mL, 559.34 mL, and 327.04 mL, respectively, and the female volumes of 590.60 mL and 330.20 mL, respectively. For the small bladders in Case II, the complete contour could be captured using only the central and lower transducers without image stitching. The calculated male bladder volumes were 88.05 mL, 7.67 mL, and 19.89 mL, respectively, while the female volumes were 22.13 mL and 126.16 mL, respectively.</p>
<figure class="fig xbox font-sm" id="fig0040"><h4 class="obj_head">Fig. 8.</h4>
<p class="img-box line-height-none margin-x-neg-2 tablet:margin-x-0 text-center"><img class="graphic" src="https://cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/blobs/c1a5/13594705/e25b668e1ca7/gr8.webp" loading="lazy" id="lk0040" height="1087" width="1600" alt="Fig. 8"></p>
<div class="p text-right font-secondary"><a href="figure/fig0040/" class="usa-link" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Open in a new tab</a></div>
<figcaption><p>Imaging results on the volunteers in Case I. (a) and (b) The ultrasound images of the distended bladders for Case I. (c) Comparison of the three-axis measurement results between the CCUA-Patch and a commercial probe.</p></figcaption></figure><figure class="fig xbox font-sm" id="fig0045"><h4 class="obj_head">Fig. 9.</h4>
<p class="img-box line-height-none margin-x-neg-2 tablet:margin-x-0 text-center"><img class="graphic" src="https://cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/blobs/c1a5/13594705/b97e30f554f9/gr9.webp" loading="lazy" id="lk0045" height="1060" width="1599" alt="Fig. 9"></p>
<div class="p text-right font-secondary"><a href="figure/fig0045/" class="usa-link" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Open in a new tab</a></div>
<figcaption><p>Imaging results on the volunteers in Case II. (a) and (b) The ultrasound images of the small bladders for Case II. (c) Comparison of the three-axis measurement results between the CCUA-Patch and a commercial probe.</p></figcaption></figure><p id="p0145"><a href="#fig0040" class="usa-link">Fig. 8</a>c and <a href="#fig0045" class="usa-link">Fig. 9</a>(c) compare the three-axis measurements between the CCUA-Patch and the Stork probe across genders and filling states. In Case I, two devices showed good consistency, though slight deviations existed between genders and individuals. Specifically, the male volunteers exhibited slightly higher measurement deviations than the females. The maximum deviation occurred in the width measurement of the male volunteer #2, where the CCUA-Patch measured 119.11 mm and the commercial probe measured 109.91 mm. However, the absolute error of 0.92 cm remained within the 1 cm threshold. In contrast, the female volunteers demonstrated higher consistency, with all measurement errors maintained below 0.5 cm. In Case II, despite the significantly smaller target size, the correlation between the two devices improved, with absolute errors for all of the five volunteers dropping below 0.2 cm across all dimensions.</p>
<p id="p0150"><a href="#fig0050" class="usa-link">Fig. 10</a> provides a quantitative comparison of bladder volume measurement results for all of the 10 volunteers. The data indicate high consistency in volume quantification, especially in small-volume scenarios. In Case II (male volunteers #4–6 and female volunteers #3–4), the volume difference was controlled within 8 mL. Notably, the absolute deviation for the female volunteer #3 was only 0.63 mL (22.13 mL vs. 22.76 mL). In Case I, while absolute errors increased with target size, relative errors remained low. For instance, the female volunteer #2 showed a difference of only 3.67 mL (1.1% relative error), and the male volunteer #2 showed 13.46 mL (2.4% relative error). The maximum deviation occurred in the male volunteer #1 (approximately 21.8%), though his three-axis dimension differences remained within 9 mm. <a href="#tbl0010" class="usa-link">Table 2</a> reports each volunteer's characteristics, CCUA-Patch and Stork measurements, volumes, and error fields.</p>
<figure class="fig xbox font-sm" id="fig0050"><h4 class="obj_head">Fig. 10.</h4>
<p class="img-box line-height-none margin-x-neg-2 tablet:margin-x-0 text-center"><img class="graphic" src="https://cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/blobs/c1a5/13594705/7224a01f6de5/gr10.webp" loading="lazy" id="lk0050" height="737" width="1600" alt="Fig. 10"></p>
<div class="p text-right font-secondary"><a href="figure/fig0050/" class="usa-link" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Open in a new tab</a></div>
<figcaption><p>Comparison of the bladder volume measurement results between the CCUA-Patch and Stork handheld probe under Case I and Case II.</p></figcaption></figure><section class="tw xbox font-sm" id="tbl0010"><h4 class="obj_head">Table 2.</h4>
