<div class="p text-right font-secondary"><a href="table/tbl0010/" class="usa-link" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Open in a new tab</a></div>
<div class="tw-foot p">
<div class="fn" id="sp0070"><p>*CCUA V and Stork V denote the bladder volumes.</p></div>
<div class="fn" id="sp0075"><p>** Error was calculated as CCUA V − Stork V.</p></div>
</div></section><p id="p0155">To further assess the agreement between the CCUA-Patch and the Stork probe, a Bland-Altman analysis was performed, as shown in <a href="#fig0055" class="usa-link">Fig. 11</a>. The horizontal axis represents the mean volume of the two devices, while the vertical axis represents the difference calculated as minus. Statistical results show a mean bias of 17.48 mL with a standard deviation of 27.1 mL, indicating that CCUA-Patch measurements are slightly higher than the commercial probe, though the overall systemic error is minimal. Most data points are distributed within the 95% Limits of Agreement (LoA), ranging from −35.63 mL to 70.6 mL. The points in the small-volume range are highly concentrated near the zero-bias line, demonstrating exceptional precision. In the large-volume range, although absolute deviations increased for certain samples due to anatomical complexity and manual operation variance, the overall trend remained stable. The scatter plot shows no significant systematic bias or proportional error resulting from increasing measurement values.</p>
<figure class="fig xbox font-sm" id="fig0055"><h4 class="obj_head">Fig. 11.</h4>
<p class="img-box line-height-none margin-x-neg-2 tablet:margin-x-0 text-center"><img class="graphic" src="https://cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/blobs/c1a5/13594705/94c9d364d4fa/gr11.webp" loading="lazy" id="lk0055" height="878" width="1247" alt="Fig. 11"></p>
<div class="p text-right font-secondary"><a href="figure/fig0055/" class="usa-link" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Open in a new tab</a></div>
<figcaption><p>Bland-Altman analysis of the bladder volume measurement results for the 10 volunteers.</p></figcaption></figure></section></section><section id="sec0055"><h2 class="pmc_sec_title">4. Discussion</h2>
<p id="p0160">The development and validation of the CCUA-Patch represent an advancement in non-invasive continuous bladder volume monitoring by addressing the inherent limitations of traditional single-probe ultrasound systems such as restricted fields of view and the requirement for manual rotation. Utilizing an innovative cross-shaped tiling layout that leverages multi-element synthetic fields of view and self-adhesive hydrogel coupling, this system effectively eliminates the truncation effects commonly encountered during the imaging of large bladder volumes. This approach ensures the complete capture of bladder morphology without the need for professional manipulation and enables the continuous measurement of bladder volume across various filling states. <a href="#tbl0015" class="usa-link">Table 3</a> compares the proposed method with those reported in the literature and indicates that the main contribution of this work lies in combining fixed dual-plane acquisition with longitudinal image stitching in a three-array patch.</p>
<section class="tw xbox font-sm" id="tbl0015"><h3 class="obj_head">Table 3.</h3>
<div class="caption p"><p>Comparison of representative ultrasound approaches for bladder-volume monitoring.</p></div>
<div class="tbl-box p" tabindex="0"><table class="content" frame="hsides" rules="groups">
<thead><tr>
<th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><strong>Study</strong></th>
<th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><strong>Mode</strong></th>
<th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><strong>Array architecture</strong></th>
<th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><strong>FOV / plane-acquisition strategy</strong></th>
<th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><strong>Principal limitation</strong></th>
</tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td colspan="1" rowspan="1">Tanaka et al., 2010 [<a href="#bib30" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="bib30">30</a>]</td>
<td colspan="1" rowspan="1">A-mode</td>
<td colspan="1" rowspan="1">12 fixed-array probes</td>
<td colspan="1" rowspan="1">Geometric segmentation and summed bladder slices</td>
<td colspan="1" rowspan="1">Bulky device; poor skin conformability</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td colspan="1" rowspan="1">Zhang et al., 2023 [<a href="#bib31" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="bib31">31</a>]</td>
<td colspan="1" rowspan="1">B-mode</td>
<td colspan="1" rowspan="1">Five 64-element phased arrays</td>
<td colspan="1" rowspan="1">Multiple probes for localization; multi-image fusion remained at phantom stage</td>
<td colspan="1" rowspan="1">Human images remained within a single-probe FOV; higher patch complexity</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td colspan="1" rowspan="1">Park et al., 2025 [<a href="#bib32" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="bib32">32</a>]</td>
<td colspan="1" rowspan="1">3D-mode</td>
<td colspan="1" rowspan="1">T-shaped cross-array probe</td>
<td colspan="1" rowspan="1">Manual shifting with motion-based 3D reconstruction</td>
<td colspan="1" rowspan="1">Manual motion; higher time and system cost</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td colspan="1" rowspan="1">This work</td>
<td colspan="1" rowspan="1">B-mode</td>
<td colspan="1" rowspan="1">Three 64-element linear arrays</td>
<td colspan="1" rowspan="1">Two fixed longitudinal arrays stitched for sagittal coverage plus one transverse array</td>
<td colspan="1" rowspan="1">Benchtop Vantage 256</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<div class="p text-right font-secondary"><a href="table/tbl0015/" class="usa-link" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Open in a new tab</a></div></section><p id="p0165">In phantom imaging validation, the CCUA-Patch achieved an imaging depth exceeding 14 cm, meeting the requirements for most bladder imaging scenarios. Furthermore, sagittal images synthesized through feature-matching showed a blind zone depth between the two arrays of approximately 1.5 cm, which is significantly shallower than typical bladder depths, thereby ensuring complete image acquisition. For tissue-mimicking phantoms, although the volume error between the CCUA-Patch and the calibrated volume reached 10.86 mL, the discrepancy compared to commercial ultrasound results was only 0.89 mL, with three-axis dimensions matching almost perfectly. This suggests that the deviation from the calibrated volume likely stems from the geometric simplification of the prolate ellipsoid formula relative to the ovoid phantom shape, rather than measurement error from the CCUA-Patch itself.</p>
<p id="p0170">Clinical validation with 10 volunteers confirmed that the CCUA-Patch maintains high measurement consistency with commercial handheld probes across different filling states. In Case I (distended bladders), the system exhibited a significant FOV advantage. Traditional single-probe linear arrays are often limited by physical aperture, frequently resulting in "truncation effects" when imaging bladders with long longitudinal diameters, which prevents capturing the complete apex or base contours. The cross layout utilizes two longitudinally arranged 64-element arrays (L1 and L2) for time-division scanning. Combined with feature-based image registration and stitching, it successfully achieves wide-field sagittal imaging. For instance, in male volunteer #2, whose longitudinal bladder span approached 10 cm, the CCUA-Patch measurements for length (91.00 mm) and height (99.24 mm) were highly consistent with the commercial convex probe (97.89 mm and 97.57 mm, respectively). This design ensures full coverage of the bladder contour, eliminating operational errors caused by clinicians frequently rotating or moving a probe to locate the maximum cross-section.</p>
<p id="p0175">For Case II (small bladders), the CCUA-Patch demonstrated exceptional precision and sensitivity. Data showed that absolute errors for the three-axis measurements in all five volunteers dropped below 0.2 cm, with the volume error for female volunteer #3 being only 0.63 mL. In contrast, handheld convex probes often struggle to maintain contact in small-volume scenarios due to their bulk and interference with the iliac bone or pelvic structures. While some distended bladder samples showed larger volume deviations (approximately 21.8%), the absolute three-axis deviations remained within 9 mm. This amplification of error is primarily due to the multiplicative effect of the prolate ellipsoid formula on individual dimensional errors, while also reflecting the limitations of manual operation. In comparative experiments, handheld probes easily shift when switching between transverse and longitudinal planes; in high-volume states, the visual differences between these planes are subtle, making it difficult to guarantee a strictly orthogonal maximum cross-section. The fixed structure of the CCUA-Patch eliminates this human uncertainty, providing a more reproducible measurement baseline.</p>
<p id="p0180">Bland-Altman analysis quantified agreement between the CCUA-Patch and the Stork clinical reference device, with a mean bias of 17.48 mL and 95% limits of agreement from −35.63–70.60 mL. Because both devices used operator-selected images and the prolate ellipsoid approximation, and neither was compared with catheterization or 3D planimetry, the observed differences cannot be attributed exclusively to either device. During large-volume measurements, manual rotation and angle adjustment can cause the operator to inadvertently select a "maximum cross-section" that occupies a different spatial position than the original plane. The CCUA-Patch, with its fixed spatial coordinate system, guarantees the orthogonality and positional stability of the transverse and longitudinal sections. Additionally, the 2 mm self-adhesive hydrogel solves the problem of traditional coupling agents drying out and requiring reapplication. Its self-adhesive nature enables hands-free monitoring, and its excellent acoustic impedance matching ensures a high signal-to-noise ratio (SNR) over hours of monitoring, significantly reducing the clinical nursing burden. This is of great value for automated bladder volume monitoring and early warning. In addition to supporting stable, hands-free coupling during the reported imaging sessions, during a preliminary device-development observation, the hydrogel remained adhered to human skin for 6 h, and no visible skin redness was observed after the attachment period. Nevertheless, this qualitative observation was not a formal dermatological safety assessment, and the maximum wear duration, delayed skin reactions, and effects of repeated exposure were not evaluated. Therefore, the current evidence supports short-term attachment under the tested conditions but does not establish long-term skin compatibility.</p>
<p id="p0185">Synthesizing the experimental results, it is evident that although the CCUA-Patch is currently constrained by the inherent errors of geometric simplification models such as the prolate ellipsoid method and exhibits individual amplified errors in large volume measurements due to anatomical variations or differences in maximum plane selection, the findings demonstrate its reliability as a substitute for traditional handheld convex array probes in long-term and automated bladder volume monitoring. Its imaging precision is sufficient to support real-time clinical assessment of bladder filling status.</p>
<p id="p0190">Overall, the CCUA-Patch exhibits high measurement accuracy in both large and small volume states, allowing the system to stably obtain the three-diameter parameters of anatomical structures and achieve automated urine volume assessment. The core advantages of the system lie in its spatial structural stability and monitoring continuity. Compared to handheld probes and certain flexible wearable patches, the structural stability of the cruciform array configuration offers distinct benefits. While traditional handheld scanning relies on the ability of the operator to locate and maintain orthogonal planes, the CCUA-Patch ensures the positional stability and orthogonality of transverse and longitudinal sections. This fixed orthogonal scanning mechanism reduces measurement random errors introduced by human operation and enhances the robustness of volume estimation. Furthermore, the ability to obtain full field-of-view and dual-section images without manual adjustment provides the fundamental basis for long-term continuous monitoring. Coupled with a self-adhesive solid hydrogel coupling layer that enables hands-free operation over extended periods, the device significantly reduces the clinical nursing burden and eliminates the need for reusable auxiliary agents, granting the system the capability for long-term monitoring without human intervention. The clinical significance of the CCUA-Patch is extensive, offering substantial practical value for the early warning of urinary retention, the evaluation of postoperative voiding function recovery, and the care management of patients with incontinence. Integrating the device host computer into mobile terminals can provide timely alerts for patients with neurogenic bladder dysfunction or impaired sensory function, thereby preventing serious complications such as vesicoureteral reflux and chronic renal failure. Additionally, the system can scientifically guide the timing of catheter removal during postoperative recovery and assist in bladder training programs to help patients regain sensory awareness, showing broad application prospects in home care and telemedicine.</p>
<p id="p0195">Despite these advantages, the current study has certain limitations that necessitate further exploration. The current in vivo experimental validation involved ten healthy volunteers with a narrow range of age and body mass index. Future research should encompass larger and more diverse populations to evaluate the performance of the system under complex anatomical and pathological conditions. Moreover, while the prolate ellipsoid formula remains the clinical standard, its geometric simplification leads to inherent errors in volume conversion, particularly as target dimensions increase. Subsequent research will focus on introducing advanced deep learning algorithms to improve the automation and precision of bladder wall segmentation. Furthermore, the patch itself is lightweight (23.07 g), compact, and hands-free after attachment, but the complete experimental system is not yet portable or wearable because image acquisition used the Vantage 256 platform. Translation toward ambulatory or home use will require a miniaturized, low-power multichannel front end, integrated beamforming or multiplexing, wireless data transfer, and a battery-powered architecture. In conclusion, the CCUA-Patch provides a robust and accurate solution for automated and non-invasive bladder volume monitoring. By balancing wearable comfort with imaging precision, it overcomes the conflict between field-of-view expansion, wearing comfort, and operational complexity inherent in portable bladder monitoring devices. This work establishes a feasible clinical alternative to traditional handheld probes and provides a new technical pathway for the automated management of lower urinary tract functions in both clinical and domestic environments. At the same time, the current prototype therefore supports feasibility only. Larger and more diverse cohorts, including target patient populations, together with an independent volume reference, and a miniaturized imaging system architecture are required before clinical or home deployment.</p>
<p id="p0200">The structural stability and unique cruciform geometry of the CCUA-Patch provide an exceptional platform for extending the system's capabilities into Photoacoustic Imaging (PAI) through a decoupled excitation-detection scheme. In this potential configuration, the CCUA-Patch serves as a dedicated, high-performance wearable receiver array, while optical excitation is provided by an external laser source. This decoupled approach offers several distinct advantages: it mitigates the thermal management challenges associated with on-patch light sources and allows for the delivery of higher-energy pulses, which are essential for achieving the penetration depths required for pelvic organ monitoring. The orthogonal layout of the CCUA-Patch is particularly well-suited for capturing photoacoustic signals generated by diffuse external illumination. By providing dual-plane acoustic reception, the system can effectively address the "limited-view" problem, enabling more accurate 3D localization and reconstruction of deep-tissue optical absorbers compared to traditional single-element or linear-array wearable sensors. Clinically, this synergy would allow the CCUA-Patch to perform continuous, non-invasive monitoring of hemodynamic parameters, such as oxygen saturation and total hemoglobin concentration within the bladder wall and surrounding pelvic tissues, during external laser therapy or diagnostic scanning. This functional insight is of paramount importance for the early detection of bladder wall ischemia, chronic inflammatory responses, and the assessment of tumor angiogenesis in bladder malignancies. Furthermore, the self-adhesive solid hydrogel layer developed in this study provides excellent optical transparency and acoustic coupling, ensuring that externally delivered light can penetrate to target depths while photoacoustic waves are efficiently transmitted back to the cruciform array. This roadmap positions the CCUA-Patch not just as a bladder volume monitor, but as a core component of a future multi-modal pelvic health surveillance system capable of identifying physiological anomalies like tissue hypoxia or abnormal angiogenesis.</p></section><section id="sec0060"><h2 class="pmc_sec_title">CRediT authorship contribution statement</h2>
<p id="p0205"><strong>Ziqi Li:</strong> Writing – original draft, Visualization, Validation, Investigation, Formal analysis, Data curation. <strong>Cong Pu:</strong> Visualization, Validation, Methodology, Investigation. <strong>Ting Zhang:</strong> Writing – review &amp; editing, Validation, Methodology. <strong>Chang Peng:</strong> Writing – review &amp; editing, Supervision, Software, Resources, Project administration, Methodology, Funding acquisition, Conceptualization.</p></section><section id="sec0065"><h2 class="pmc_sec_title">Ethics declaration</h2>
<p id="p0210">Written informed consent to take part in the study and to publish the article has been obtained from all participants or their legal representatives. The privacy rights of participants have been observed.</p>
<p id="p0215">This study was performed in compliance with relevant laws, regulatory frameworks and guidelines where the research took place. This study was approved by the ShanghaiTech University (Approval No. Q2023-020).</p></section><section id="coi0005"><h2 class="pmc_sec_title">Declaration of Competing Interest</h2>
<p id="p0220">The authors declare that they have no known competing financial interests or personal relationships that could have appeared to influence the work reported in this paper.</p></section><section id="ack0005" class="ack"><h2 class="pmc_sec_title">Acknowledgements</h2>
<p id="p0225">This work was supported in part by the National Natural Science Foundation of China (Grant No. 12204307).</p></section><section id="_bios93_" lang="en"><h2 class="pmc_sec_title">Biographies</h2>
<section id="bio1" class="bio"><p class="img-box line-height-none margin-x-neg-2 tablet:margin-x-0 text-center"><img class="graphic" src="https://cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/blobs/c1a5/13594705/890099d5779d/fx1.webp" loading="lazy" id="lk0065" height="665" width="499" alt="graphic file with name fx1.webp"></p>
<p><strong>Ziqi Li</strong> received the B.S. degree in biomedical engineering from Capital Medical University, Beijing, China, in 2024. She is currently pursuing a master’s degree with the Department of Biomedical Engineering, ShanghaiTech University, Shanghai, China. Her research interests mainly focus on the ultrasound imaging and flexible and wearable ultrasound devices.</p></section><section id="bio2" class="bio"><p class="img-box line-height-none margin-x-neg-2 tablet:margin-x-0 text-center"><img class="graphic" src="https://cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/blobs/c1a5/13594705/1b5577296dc0/fx2.webp" loading="lazy" id="lk0070" height="665" width="499" alt="graphic file with name fx2.webp"></p>
<p><strong>Cong Pu</strong> received the B.E. degree in biomedical engineering from the University of Shanghai for Science and Technology, Shanghai, China, in 2022. He is currently pursuing the Ph.D. degree in electronic science and technology from the School of Biomedical Engineering, ShanghaiTech University, Shanghai. His current research interests include wearable ultrasound devices and ultrasound neuromodulation.</p></section><section id="bio3" class="bio"><p class="img-box line-height-none margin-x-neg-2 tablet:margin-x-0 text-center"><img class="graphic" src="https://cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/blobs/c1a5/13594705/6a42174ba1bf/fx3.webp" loading="lazy" id="lk0075" height="665" width="499" alt="graphic file with name fx3.webp"></p>
<p><strong>Ting Zhang</strong> is a doctoral candidate in the Department of Integrative Medicine, Huashan Hospital, Fudan University, under the supervision of Professor Yanqing Wang. Her research focuses on acupuncture for refractory chronic diseases and integrative Chinese–Western medicine in the diagnosis, treatment, and mechanistic study of malignant tumors. She has led one National Natural Science Foundation of China Youth Program project and one Huashan Hospital Basic Research Youth Cultivation Program, and has participated in several national and provincial research projects. She has published seven papers in recent years and contributed to textbooks and guidelines including Schools of Chinese Acupuncture and Moxibustion. She currently serves as a member of several academic committees, including relevant CACA professional committees and the Shanghai TCM Oncology Branch.</p></section><section id="bio4" class="bio"><p class="img-box line-height-none margin-x-neg-2 tablet:margin-x-0 text-center"><img class="graphic" src="https://cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/blobs/c1a5/13594705/b5ea66f0d2ad/fx4.webp" loading="lazy" id="lk0080" height="665" width="499" alt="graphic file with name fx4.webp"></p>
<p><strong>Chang Peng</strong>, Ph.D., is an Assistant Professor and Ph.D. advisor at the School of Biomedical Engineering, ShanghaiTech University. He received his Ph.D. in Mechanical Engineering from the University of Florida in 2018 and conducted postdoctoral research at North Carolina State University from 2018 to 2021. His research mainly focuses on miniaturized medical ultrasound transducers and wearable ultrasound devices. He has published over 40 journal papers, filed 17 U.S. and Chinese invention patents, and led or participated in seven national, provincial, and industry-funded projects. His work has been featured by IEEE Spectrum, Forbes, and other media. He also serves on the IEEE IUS Technical Program Committee and other professional committees.</p></section></section><section id="da0005"><h2 class="pmc_sec_title">Data availability</h2>
